From f9746a61495e96fda7bbbc19dd288ecadd8f4aa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:15:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/46] Keep word timings when the lenient TTML parser is used

BetterLyrics returns documents OuterTune's strict TTML2 reader rejects, so
those go through the lenient parser in the betterlyrics module instead. That
parser has always extracted word timings - it needs them to reconstruct a
line's text out of syllable spans - but toLrc() then threw them away and
emitted one timestamp per line, so a song recovered that way could only ever
be highlighted line by line.

toLrc() gains an enhanced mode that writes the words back out as Enhanced LRC
("the A2 extension"): a <mm:ss.cc> sync point before each word, plus one after
the last word to pin its end time rather than leaving the reader to estimate
it from a characters-per-second average.

Enhanced LRC rather than a schema change because lyrics are stored as a plain
string. Nobody takes a Room migration, a line-level reader still sees ordinary
LRC because it ignores the <...> marks, and providers that return plain LRC
need no special casing - they simply have no marks. A line the encoding cannot
represent safely (a word containing <, >, [ or ], or timings that run
backwards) is written line-level, so a mixed document degrades per line.

Plain mode is byte-identical to the previous output, and the tests assert that
stripping every word mark from the enhanced form gives it back.
