From 91a8c27bace9cfd1a236824ad3869fe1e8f86192 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:25:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/46] Rewrite the word-by-word lyrics sweep

The karaoke path measured nothing and guessed everything. Progress across a
line was the fraction of words completed, so the highlight advanced in equal
steps regardless of how wide each word actually was and drifted away from the
voice on any line with uneven words. A wrapped line was handled by re-splitting
the text on spaces and slicing the word list by the resulting word counts,
which is only correct when the two agree; when they did not, words.first() on
an empty slice threw, and a slice whose start ran past the end of the list threw
before that. It also ignored the lyric alignment setting, so a wrapping
line was left-aligned mid-song and jumped back to centre when it finished.

Replaced with a renderer that asks the text layout where the characters are.
The line is measured once, the playback position becomes a fractional character
offset, and TextLayoutResult.getBoundingBox turns that into an x coordinate.
Wrapping then needs no special case at all - a character on the third visual
line reports a box on the third visual line - and shaping, kerning and
alignment are whatever Text would have produced, because it is the same layout.
The sweep itself is the bright text masked by a gradient at the boundary, so it
reads as a light moving across the words rather than words changing colour.

Two performance changes come with it. The position is read inside the draw
lambda, so the clock ticking sixty times a second invalidates drawing and not
composition; previously it was read during composition, which recomposed every
visible lyric on every tick. And only the current line and the next one are
drawn this way, which also removes the visible swap when a line becomes
current, since an unswept line is drawn exactly as the plain path draws an
upcoming one.

The sweep is a pure function of the player's position rather than an animation
running alongside it, so a seek lands where it should instead of unwinding from
wherever the animation had got to. Between the player's own coarse position
updates the wall clock carries it, re-latching whenever the player reports a
new position.

Also: the power-saver check was a binder call per lyric line per recomposition
and is now made once; the scroll refresh no longer skips its whole tick while
paused, so scrubbing moves the highlight; and lyricRefreshRate was a local
reassigned from inside a LaunchedEffect that had already captured it, which
never took effect.
