From 4ab3440c614b49521e4f3052d5c230a9cabdf632 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:33:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 35/46] Let the user choose which lyric provider is preferred

Lyrics came from whichever of the six providers answered first with a synced
result. That is the fastest way to get lyrics and it is the right default, but
it gives the user no way to say "I want LrcLib's version of this song", and no
way to keep a provider they dislike from winning simply because it is quick.

Two settings, in the lyric sources section:

Provider selection, Automatic or In my order. Automatic is exactly today's
behaviour. In my order asks the providers one at a time from the top and takes
the first synced result. It is slower on purpose - that is the trade being
offered, and racing the tail of the list to hide it would defeat the point.

Provider priority, a drag-to-reorder list. The order is stored as a comma-
joined list of provider ids. Ids that name nothing are ignored and providers
the stored order does not mention keep their built-in position and go last, so
a stored order written before a provider existed leaves the newcomer at the end
rather than hiding it, and one written before a provider was removed still
works. Reordering is only written back when the dialog is confirmed, so an
abandoned drag does not silently change which provider answers.

The order also decides how the alternate-lyrics picker lists its candidates,
which is useful even in automatic mode - so the reorder list is available in
both, and the dialog says which of the two it is currently affecting.

providerSignature now carries the mode and the enabled ids, so a cached miss
recorded under one configuration is not served under another. The ids are in
the user's order in manual mode, where order changes which provider answers,
and sorted in automatic mode, where it does not - reordering there should not
throw away caches that are still perfectly valid.

Going in order also needs its own time budget. The racing cap of 12 seconds
applies to all providers at once; applied to a sequential walk it would mean
the last entries in the user's order were simply never asked. The budget is
per-provider instead, and a provider that runs out of time reports nothing at
all, which leaves the result indeterminate rather than writing a negative cache
the providers below it never got to contradict.
