From d00b3a1902979e3254bf476030eb7664044ed8cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 02:48:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 43/46] Prefer word-timed lyrics over whichever provider
 answers first

The word-by-word sweep was never engaging, and the renderer was not the reason:
the lyrics it was handed had no word timings in them.

Providers are raced and the first synced result won. In practice that is
whichever provider is quickest, not whichever one carries word timings - so a
song whose lyrics exist in both forms got the line-level version essentially
every time, and there was nothing for the sweep to sweep. BetterLyrics returns
Apple-style TTML with per-word timing and is one of the slower providers, which
is close to the worst case for a race decided on arrival order.

Word timings are now their own outcome, ranked above plain synced. A word-timed
result still ends the race immediately, since nothing better can arrive. A
line-level one is held as a fallback instead of adopted, so a slower provider
gets its chance to answer with the better form, bounded by the same overall
timeout as before. Nothing is lost when no provider has word timings: the held
result is adopted exactly as it would have been.

This also makes the provider-order setting mean something for karaoke: in
manual mode, putting BetterLyrics first now decides the outcome rather than
being overtaken.

One existing test asserted the old contract - that a synced result stops the
race - and now asserts that it does not, with the reason written down.
