From 6e360d501d909052eaaa8771edd0a8ad9c862777 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 03:28:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 45/46] Actually place the blocks an imported layout asks for

The importer read every field in the file, validated it, showed it back in the
settings summary, and then the player used two of them: whether a block is
visible, and the order of title, progress and controls among themselves.
Position, rotation, scale, spacing and the action row were parsed and dropped.

Free placement now works. The five placeable blocks are drawn into one box the
size of the player, each measured at its own width percentage and positioned so
its centre lands on its coordinates - centre because that is what the editor
writes from schema 3 on, and because it is the only anchor that keeps a block
where you put it when you resize it. Later blocks draw over earlier ones where
they overlap, which is the order the file lists them in.

Getting there needed the cover to move. It was drawn by PortraitPlayer in a
weighted box above the controls, which is a different coordinate space from
everything else, so no coordinate could ever place it relative to them. In free
placement PortraitPlayer hands it to ControlsContent as a block instead, and
the whole player becomes the one space they share. Stacked layouts are drawn
exactly as before.

Scale and rotation are graphics-layer transforms rather than a re-layout, so a
scaled block gets visually bigger without reflowing its own text or shoving its
neighbours, which is both what the editor previews and what keeps a block where
its coordinates say it will be.

The action row is a block now. In free placement it is placed like any other; in
a stacked layout it stays inline with the title where it has always been, and
the file can only hide it - moving it there would change the look for everyone
who has never imported anything.

Two things a layout still cannot do, both deliberate. The queue is a bottom
sheet anchored to the bottom of the screen, so it has nothing to position; only
its visibility is read. And free placement turns off swipe to skip, because
that gesture is a full-width pager and a cover that can be any width, anywhere,
at any angle is not one.
